# agentbob secrets — copy this file to .env (same directory) and fill in.
# This file lives in $BOB_HOME (default ~/.bob/). .env is gitignored — never commit it.
# Only secrets belong here; non-secret settings go in config.yaml.

# ---- IM platforms ----

# Telegram bot token from @BotFather (needed for `bob gateway`):
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# Feishu / Lark (自建应用 over WebSocket). Setting BOTH vars enables the source.
#   1. open.feishu.cn → 创建企业自建应用 → copy App ID + App Secret
#   2. 应用功能 → 机器人 → enable bot ability
#   3. 权限管理 → add im:message, im:message.group_at_msg, im:message:send_as_bot
#      (im:resource + im:message.reaction:write for later attachment/reaction PRs)
#   4. 事件订阅 → choose 长连接/WebSocket mode → subscribe im.message.receive_v1
#   5. fill both lines below, restart bob, then DM the bot or @it in a group
# See docs/sources-feishu-design.md §8 for the full console checklist.
# FEISHU_APP_ID=cli_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# FEISHU_APP_SECRET=

# Future IM channels — uncomment + fill in when those sources land:
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# IMESSAGE_TOKEN=
# WECOM_SECRET=

# ---- Email source (IMAP+SMTP) ----
# Multi-account inbound + outbound. Each account has:
#   - a per-account SOURCE_ID (appears as ev.Source — e.g. "email-support")
#   - one APP PASSWORD (NOT the account login password — generate one)
#   - allowlist + admin list (configured in $BOB_HOME/sources/email.yaml)
#
# Setup:
#   1. start bob once — it creates $BOB_HOME/sources/email.yaml as a stub
#   2. edit that yaml to enable: true + add accounts list + allowlist
#   3. for each account's pass_env: field, add a matching *_PASS line below
#
# Gmail App Password:
#   1. enable 2FA on the Google account
#   2. https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords  →  generate 16-char password
#   3. paste it as the EMAIL_<NAME>_PASS value (NOT the account password)
# Outlook / Microsoft 365:
#   1. https://account.microsoft.com/security  →  app passwords
#   2. paste likewise
# Self-hosted (Dovecot / Postfix etc): use the account's IMAP/SMTP login password.
#
# Each line below matches one account's pass_env: in email.yaml.
# EMAIL_SUPPORT_PASS=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
# EMAIL_SALES_PASS=yyyy-yyyy-yyyy-yyyy
# EMAIL_DEV_PASS=zzzz-zzzz-zzzz-zzzz
#
# closed-default: with empty allowlist in email.yaml, NOTHING is accepted —
# nothing happens until admin adds trusted senders. See docs/email-source-design.md.

# ---- Model provider keys ----
# Only set the one your model.provider / model.api_key_env actually points at:
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---- PostgreSQL (optional; required when store.backend=postgres or fallback) ----
# Set store.backend in config.yaml to "postgres" (or "fallback" for pg+sqlite
# dual) and put the libpq DSN here. The name (BOB_POSTGRES_DSN) is the default;
# rename via store.postgres.dsn_env if you prefer another env var name.
# Recommended params: ?connect_timeout=2&statement_timeout=10000&pool_max_conns=10
# (short connect_timeout keeps fallback failover fast.)
# BOB_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=disable&connect_timeout=2

# ---- webui dashboard ----
# docker-compose.yml already sets BOB_WEBUI_ADDR=0.0.0.0:9877 for the container
# (a 127.0.0.1 bind there is unreachable by the port-forward AND the cloudflared
# sidecar). Override only to move the port or disable the server:
# BOB_WEBUI_ADDR=0.0.0.0:9877   # "off" disables the http server (registry still provided)

# ---- Cloudflare Tunnel (optional; fronts the webui with public HTTPS) ----
# The cloudflared service in docker-compose.yml runs a token tunnel — no inbound
# port is opened. Setup: Cloudflare Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → create a
# tunnel → add a public hostname routed to service http://bob:9877 → copy the
# tunnel token here. Leave unset to skip public access (the LAN port-forward still
# works); cloudflared restart-loops without it, so comment that service out if
# you don't use a tunnel.
# TUNNEL_TOKEN=
